# Line endings are normalised to LF in the repository and in the working
# tree, on every platform. Two reasons, both found the hard way on
# 18 October 2026:
#
#   * `tests/test_api.py` writes a stand-in build and then compares the
#     bytes the server returns with the text it wrote. With CRLF in the
#     working tree those differ on Windows, and two tests failed for a
#     reason that had nothing to do with the server. The test now writes
#     with `newline=""`; this file removes the trap rather than the
#     symptom, for the next fixture nobody thinks about.
#   * With `core.autocrlf=true` and no rules here, Git kept reporting
#     files as staged that nothing had staged, whenever a tool wrote them
#     with LF into a CRLF working tree.
#
# CI runs on Linux, so LF is what the tested bytes are anyway. An editor
# on Windows handles LF files without help.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files Git must never translate. A byte changed inside a font, an image
# or a workbook corrupts it, and `text=auto` guesses from content.
*.ttf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.xlsx binary
*.mp4 binary
*.zip binary
